Aww, don't be so harsh. The early game's repetition is counteracted by
the fact that it intrigues the player with _percieved potential_ - the
idea that just around the next corner could be an item that will
dramatically(?) transform the state of play. Early speed or jumping
boots, or a teleport control ring, and such.

>The topic is about making Nethack harder.  The discussion is
>about making Ascension easier by making it shorter and more
>interesting.

>Don't be fooled by labels.

I don't know about _shorter_, per se. Most people say "shorter" simply
because it's seemingly equivalent to "less mazes." And (assumedly)
people say "easier" simply because it's seemingly equivalent to
"shorter."

Here are some points in which I believe:

1) What we all really want is for Gehennom (and, in the end, only
Gehennom) to be more interesting.
2) Gameplay is interesting.
3) And in the early game, player vulnerability -> gameplay.
4) It is rational to believe that 3 will also hold for the middle game.
5) Therefore, if the player is more vulnerable in Gehennom, Gehennom is
more interesting - even before we deal with the antipathy toward the
mazes.
But...
6) The problem is how to ensure that this vulnerability is spread
equally among roles and player abilities, such that under-powered
players don't become unplayable.



